---
// What buying new costs you in floor area, on your own budget.
//
// The returns argument about new launch versus resale cannot be settled on the
// evidence that exists (that is the article's point). This is the part that can:
// the price difference on the day you sign, expressed as the thing a buyer
// actually feels — how much home the same money reaches.
//
// TWO OF OUR OWN MEASURES, AND THEY ARE NOT THE SAME MEASURE. Stated plainly in
// the footnote because combining them is the one soft step here:
//
//   gapPct  — median of new-sale $psf against the resale median in the SAME
//             district and SAME quarter, within size band and tenure class.
//             1,181 quarterly comparisons, 2021Q3–2026Q3, URA caveats.
//
//             CORRECTED 2026-08-09. These were 34.4 / 62.1 / 82.7 on n=493/547/333.
//             That run keyed its comparator pool on a district field URA carries on
//             the transaction, not on the project, so every district merged into one
//             national pool — which flattered CCR (priced against cheap suburban
//             resale) and understated OCR (priced against dear central resale). The
//             segment order reverses. See sg-property-decision
//             reviews/2026-08-09_lease-matched-entry-gap.md.
//
//             Not matched on lease. Restricting the resale side to 85+ years
//             remaining takes all three to about +30%, so roughly a third of what is
//             shown here is vintage rather than newness.
//   psf     — the segment's own resale median $psf over the last 12 months,
//             URA transactions, all sizes and tenures pooled.
//
// The gap is a like-for-like comparison; the median is a broad aggregate. Applying
// one to the other is an illustration of scale, not a valuation of any unit. The
// footnote says so rather than letting the precision imply otherwise.
//
// NOT a measure of overpaying: the comparison is against every existing home in
// the district, including thirty-year-old stock, so part of the gap buys a
// genuinely newer building. The component must never imply otherwise.
const SEGMENTS = [
  { key: 'OCR', name: 'Outside Central', gapPct: 52.0, n: 405, psf: 1872 },
  { key: 'RCR', name: 'Rest of Central', gapPct: 46.9, n: 483, psf: 2362 },
  { key: 'CCR', name: 'Core Central',    gapPct: 42.2, n: 293, psf: 2756 },
];

const DEFAULT_BUDGET = 1_500_000;
const fmt0 = (n: number) => Math.round(n).toLocaleString('en-SG');

// Widest resale area across segments sets the scale, so the bars stay comparable
// between rows instead of each row scaling to itself.
const maxSqft = Math.max(...SEGMENTS.map((s) => DEFAULT_BUDGET / s.psf));
---

<figure class="eg" data-eg>
  <figcaption class="eg-cap">
    <h4>What the same budget reaches, new versus existing</h4>
    <p>New-build homes cost more per square foot than existing ones nearby. This is what
    that difference costs you in floor area, on a budget you choose. Computed by us from
    URA transaction records.</p>
  </figcaption>

  <div class="eg-input">
    <label for="eg-budget">Your budget</label>
    <div class="eg-field">
      <i>S$</i>
      <input
        id="eg-budget"
        type="text"
        inputmode="numeric"
        autocomplete="off"
        value={fmt0(DEFAULT_BUDGET)}
        aria-describedby="eg-note"
      />
    </div>
  </div>
  {/* The slider is EASED, not linear. A linear track from S$300k to S$5m spends most
      of its travel above S$2.5m, where almost nobody is shopping, and gives coarse
      control across S$1m–S$2m, where most of this market actually sits. The square
      curve puts roughly S$1.5m at the midpoint. Same approach as EstateExposureTool. */}
  <input class="eg-range" type="range" id="eg-range" min="0" max="1000" step="1"
         value="505" aria-label="Budget" />
  <div class="eg-ticks" aria-hidden="true"><span>S$300k</span><span>S$1.5m</span><span>S$5m</span></div>

  <div class="eg-rows">
    {SEGMENTS.map((s) => {
      const resale = DEFAULT_BUDGET / s.psf;
      const nw = DEFAULT_BUDGET / (s.psf * (1 + s.gapPct / 100));
      return (
        <div class="eg-row" data-seg={s.key} data-gap={s.gapPct} data-psf={s.psf}>
          <div class="eg-name">
            <b>{s.name}</b>
            <span>new costs <em data-gaplabel>+{s.gapPct.toFixed(0)}%</em> per sq ft</span>
          </div>
          <div class="eg-bars">
            <div class="eg-bar-row">
              <span class="eg-key"><i class="eg-sw resale"></i>Existing</span>
              <div class="eg-track">
                <div class="eg-bar resale" data-bar="resale" style={`width:${(resale / maxSqft) * 100}%`}></div>
              </div>
              <span class="eg-val" data-val="resale">{fmt0(resale)}</span>
            </div>
            <div class="eg-bar-row">
              <span class="eg-key"><i class="eg-sw new"></i>New</span>
              <div class="eg-track">
                <div class="eg-bar new" data-bar="new" style={`width:${(nw / maxSqft) * 100}%`}></div>
              </div>
              <span class="eg-val" data-val="new">{fmt0(nw)}</span>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="eg-diff">
            <b data-diff>{fmt0(resale - nw)}</b>
            <span>sq ft less</span>
          </div>
        </div>
      );
    })}
  </div>

  <p class="eg-foot" id="eg-note">Square feet, at each segment&rsquo;s own resale median.
  The gap is <b>not</b> a measure of overpaying &mdash; it is measured against every existing
  home in the district, including stock built thirty years ago, so part of it buys a
  genuinely newer building. What it does tell you is what the same money reaches.</p>

  <div class="eg-scroll">
    <table class="eg-table">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Segment</th>
          <th>New costs more per sq ft</th>
          <th>Resale median $psf</th>
          <th>Comparisons</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {SEGMENTS.map((s) => (
          <tr>
            <td>{s.name}</td>
            <td>+{s.gapPct.toFixed(1)}%</td>
            <td>${fmt0(s.psf)}</td>
            <td>{fmt0(s.n)}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p class="eg-src">Sources, both ours, and not the same measure: the <b>gap</b> is the median
  of new-sale $psf against the resale median in the same district and quarter, within size band
  and tenure class &mdash; 1,373 quarterly comparisons, 2021Q3&ndash;2026Q3. The <b>resale median
  $psf</b> is each segment&rsquo;s own 12-month median across all sizes and tenures. Applying one
  to the other shows the scale of the difference; it is not a valuation of any particular unit.
  Both from URA caveat data.</p>
</figure>

<style is:global>
.eg{
  --eg-resale:#2563EB;                   /* validated categorical pair, light surface */
  --eg-new:#B45309;
  --eg-rule:var(--color-border); --eg-ink:var(--color-dark);
  --eg-body:var(--color-body); --eg-muted:var(--color-muted);
  --eg-mono:var(--font-mono),'SF Mono',Menlo,Consolas,monospace;
  margin:26px 0;padding:20px 22px 16px;background:#fff;
  border:1px solid var(--eg-rule);border-radius:12px
}
/* No dark-scheme colour swap. The block here was re-stepped for a dark card
   that never arrives: this card sets background:#fff unconditionally, so those
   lighter values landed on white at 3.63:1 and 3.29:1. Every colour here is
   measured against white — #2563EB 5.17:1, #B45309 5.02:1 — and stays put. */
.post-content .eg-cap{margin:0 0 16px;padding:0;text-align:left}
.post-content .eg-cap h4{margin:0 0 3px;font-size:15.5px;font-weight:700;color:var(--eg-ink);
  font-family:var(--font-body);letter-spacing:0}
.post-content .eg-cap p{margin:0;font-size:12.5px;color:var(--eg-muted);line-height:1.5}

.eg-input{display:flex;align-items:center;gap:10px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:18px}
.post-content .eg-input label{font-size:12.5px;color:var(--eg-muted);font-weight:600;margin:0}
.eg-field{display:inline-flex;align-items:center;gap:4px;border:1px solid var(--eg-rule);
  border-radius:8px;padding:6px 10px;background:#fff}
.eg-field i{font-style:normal;font-size:13px;color:var(--eg-muted)}
/* type=text + inputmode, not type=number: the spec requires a valid floating-point
   number, so a comma makes the value invalid and the browser silently discards it. */
.eg-field input{border:0;outline:0;background:none;width:10ch;font-family:var(--eg-mono);
  font-variant-numeric:tabular-nums;font-size:14.5px;font-weight:600;color:var(--eg-ink)}
.eg-field:focus-within{border-color:var(--eg-resale);box-shadow:0 0 0 2px rgba(37,99,235,.12)}

.eg-range{-webkit-appearance:none;appearance:none;width:100%;height:4px;
  background:var(--eg-rule);border-radius:999px;outline:none;cursor:pointer;
  padding:0;border:none;margin:0 0 6px;display:block}
.eg-range::-webkit-slider-thumb{-webkit-appearance:none;width:18px;height:18px;border-radius:50%;
  background:var(--eg-resale);border:2px solid #fff;box-shadow:0 1px 3px rgba(0,0,0,.18);cursor:grab}
.eg-range::-moz-range-thumb{width:14px;height:14px;border-radius:50%;
  background:var(--eg-resale);border:2px solid #fff}
.eg-range:focus-visible{outline:2px solid var(--eg-resale);outline-offset:4px}
.eg-ticks{display:flex;justify-content:space-between;font-size:10.5px;color:var(--eg-muted);
  font-family:var(--eg-mono);margin-bottom:16px}

.eg-rows{display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:14px}
.eg-row{display:grid;grid-template-columns:140px 1fr 84px;gap:14px;align-items:center;
  padding-bottom:14px;border-bottom:1px solid var(--eg-rule)}
.eg-row:last-child{border-bottom:0;padding-bottom:0}
.eg-name{display:flex;flex-direction:column;line-height:1.3}
.eg-name b{font-size:13.5px;color:var(--eg-ink);font-weight:700}
.eg-name span{font-size:11.5px;color:var(--eg-muted)}
.eg-name em{font-style:normal;font-weight:700;color:var(--eg-new)}

.eg-bars{display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:5px}
.eg-bar-row{display:grid;grid-template-columns:66px 1fr 58px;gap:8px;align-items:center}
.eg-key{font-size:11px;color:var(--eg-muted);display:flex;align-items:center;gap:5px}
.eg-sw{width:9px;height:9px;border-radius:2px;flex:none;display:inline-block}
.eg-sw.resale{background:var(--eg-resale)} .eg-sw.new{background:var(--eg-new)}
.eg-track{position:relative;height:16px}
.eg-bar{position:absolute;left:0;top:0;height:16px;border-radius:0 3px 3px 0;
  transition:width .35s ease;min-width:2px}
.eg-bar.resale{background:var(--eg-resale)} .eg-bar.new{background:var(--eg-new)}
.eg-val{font-family:var(--eg-mono);font-variant-numeric:tabular-nums;font-size:12.5px;
  font-weight:600;color:var(--eg-ink);text-align:right}

.eg-diff{text-align:right;line-height:1.2}
.eg-diff b{display:block;font-family:var(--eg-mono);font-variant-numeric:tabular-nums;
  font-size:16px;font-weight:700;color:var(--eg-new)}
.eg-diff span{font-size:10.5px;color:var(--eg-muted)}

.post-content .eg-foot{font-size:13.5px;color:var(--eg-body);line-height:1.6;margin:16px 0 0}
.eg-foot b{color:var(--eg-ink);font-weight:600}
.eg-scroll{overflow-x:auto;margin-top:14px}
.post-content table.eg-table{width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;font-size:13.5px;margin:0}
.post-content .eg-table th{font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.06em;text-transform:uppercase;
  color:var(--eg-muted);font-weight:700;background:none;padding:0 10px 6px 0;
  border-bottom:1px solid var(--eg-rule);text-align:left}
.post-content .eg-table th:not(:first-child),
.post-content .eg-table td:not(:first-child){text-align:right;font-family:var(--eg-mono);
  font-variant-numeric:tabular-nums}
.post-content .eg-table td{padding:7px 10px 7px 0;border-bottom:1px solid var(--eg-rule);
  color:var(--eg-body)}
.post-content .eg-table tr:last-child td{border-bottom:none}
.post-content .eg-src{font-size:11.5px;color:var(--eg-muted);line-height:1.55;margin:12px 0 0}
.eg-src b{color:var(--eg-body);font-weight:600}

@media(max-width:600px){
  .eg{padding:16px 12px 13px}
  .eg-row{grid-template-columns:1fr 70px;gap:8px 10px}
  .eg-name{grid-column:1/-1}
  .eg-bars{grid-column:1}
  .eg-bar-row{grid-template-columns:52px 1fr 52px;gap:6px}
  .eg-key{font-size:10px}
  .eg-val{font-size:11.5px}
  .eg-diff b{font-size:14px}
  /* a 4px track is hard to grab on a phone; pad the hit area without moving the line */
  .eg-range{height:6px;padding:14px 0;background-clip:content-box}
}
@media(prefers-reduced-motion:reduce){ .eg-bar{transition:none} }
</style>

<script is:inline>
  (function () {
    var root = document.querySelector('[data-eg]');
    if (!root) return;
    var input = root.querySelector('#eg-budget');
    var rows = Array.prototype.slice.call(root.querySelectorAll('.eg-row'));

    var range = root.querySelector('#eg-range');
    var MIN = 300000, MAX = 5000000, CURVE = 2;

    // Every read goes through this: +"1,500,000" is NaN.
    function numOf(v) {
      var n = parseFloat(String(v).replace(/[^0-9.]/g, ''));
      return isFinite(n) ? n : 0;
    }
    var fmt = function (n) { return Math.round(n).toLocaleString('en-SG'); };

    // Snap to figures a person would actually say out loud.
    function roundNice(v) {
      var step = v < 1000000 ? 10000 : 25000;
      return Math.round(v / step) * step;
    }
    function posToVal(p) { return roundNice(MIN + (MAX - MIN) * Math.pow(p / 1000, CURVE)); }
    function valToPos(v) {
      var c = Math.min(Math.max(v, MIN), MAX);
      return Math.round(1000 * Math.pow((c - MIN) / (MAX - MIN), 1 / CURVE));
    }

    function render() {
      var budget = numOf(input.value);
      // One scale across all rows so the segments stay comparable to each other.
      var max = 0;
      rows.forEach(function (r) { max = Math.max(max, budget / numOf(r.dataset.psf)); });
      if (!max) max = 1;

      rows.forEach(function (r) {
        var psf = numOf(r.dataset.psf), gap = numOf(r.dataset.gap);
        var resale = budget / psf;
        var nw = budget / (psf * (1 + gap / 100));
        r.querySelector('[data-bar="resale"]').style.width = (resale / max) * 100 + '%';
        r.querySelector('[data-bar="new"]').style.width = (nw / max) * 100 + '%';
        r.querySelector('[data-val="resale"]').textContent = fmt(resale);
        r.querySelector('[data-val="new"]').textContent = fmt(nw);
        r.querySelector('[data-diff]').textContent = fmt(resale - nw);
      });
    }

    range.addEventListener('input', function () {
      input.value = fmt(posToVal(+range.value));
      render();
    });
    input.addEventListener('input', function () {
      range.value = valToPos(numOf(input.value));
      render();
    });
    input.addEventListener('blur', function () {
      var n = numOf(input.value);
      input.value = n ? fmt(n) : '';
      render();
    });
    render();
  })();
</script>
